// ribbon_emissive.mat — Epic 2 Task 5 (VM-023). The BEHAVIOR path ribbon's
// material: the ref-1 "green hero glow" bloom, fed by palette.ribbon_core/
// ribbon_glow + emissive.ribbon_strength. Bloom is already enabled in
// renderer.cpp (BloomOptions, set up in Epic 1 precisely so this task needs
// no renderer change to glow) -- this material only has to emit above the
// bloom threshold.
//
// OPAQUE (flicker root-cause fix, 2026-09-10): this was `blending : fade`
// so the BEHAVIOR ribbon could fade in place via its own alpha -- but that
// parked the hero ribbon PERMANENTLY in Filament's blended queue, which
// sorts per-renderable and writes no depth. Co-located with the (also
// blended) velocity ribbon on the same spine, whichever strip was rebuilt
// most recently drew LAST and fully overpainted the other at alpha 1 --
// the exact binary teal on/off toggle of the live flicker report
// (test_ribbon_dropout.cpp's BehaviorRibbonNeverVanishesUnderCoLocated-
// CarpetChurn reproduces it against the fade version of this file).
// Opaque writes depth, so the z-stagger (BEHAVIOR 0.058 above velocity
// 0.052) decides visibility per-pixel regardless of draw order -- the same
// reason fresh GLOBAL/LOCAL (opaque clay.mat) never flickered. Staleness
// now fades via the clay_translucent.mat instance swap, the identical
// mechanism GLOBAL/LOCAL always used (ribbon.cpp); the emissive glow is
// not carried through the 1s death-fade (clay_translucent has no emissive
// param), an accepted tradeoff -- both shipped themes author
// emissive.ribbon_strength 0.0 today anyway.
//
// baseColor stays float4 (alpha ignored by the opaque pipeline) so
// renderer.cpp's theme-apply writes need no signature change.
//
// NO COLOR VERTEX ATTRIBUTE DEMANDED: this ribbon's geometry comes from
// extrude_polyline() (polyline.hpp), which emits position + tangent-frame
// only -- no COLOR attribute, and must not grow one just to satisfy a
// material's `requires:` line (clay_faded.mat's mistake, documented on
// that file). So: no `requires:` line at all, exactly like clay.mat.
//
// LIT (not unlit): the glow reads as an EMISSIVE surface catching bloom, not
// a flat self-lit decal -- `emissive.rgb` here is what Filament's bloom pass
// actually keys off of (values driven above the tonemapper's 1.0 by
// ribbonStrength read as a multiplier, not a fixed unlit color), and LIT
// still gets a baseColor contribution from the scene's own lighting so the
// ribbon doesn't go pitch-black if bloom/bloomStrength were ever tuned to
// zero.
material {
    name : ribbon_emissive,
    shadingModel : lit,
    parameters : [
        { type : float4, name : baseColor },
        { type : float, name : roughness },
        { type : float, name : metallic },
        { type : float3, name : emissiveColor },
        { type : float, name : emissiveStrength }
    ]
}
fragment {
    void material(inout MaterialInputs material) {
        prepareMaterial(material);
        material.baseColor = materialParams.baseColor;
        material.roughness = materialParams.roughness;
        material.metallic = materialParams.metallic;
        material.emissive.rgb = materialParams.emissiveColor * materialParams.emissiveStrength;
        material.emissive.a = 0.0;  // Filament convention: emissive.a is NOT an alpha multiplier
    }
}
